DT_CHOSEN_MT_PERSIST_COUNTER := meshtastic,persist-counter

config MESHTASTIC_CLOCK_PERSIST
	bool "Carry the wall clock across a warm reset"
	default y
	depends on MESHTASTIC_BOOTLOG
	depends on $(dt_chosen_enabled,$(DT_CHOSEN_MT_PERSIST_COUNTER))
	select COUNTER
	help
	  Keep the epoch in retained RAM and bring it forward across a reset,
	  using a free-running counter to measure how long the node was down.

	  This closes the largest gap in the port's time handling. Acquiring the
	  time is well covered — GNSS, SNTP, a phone, a peer — but every one of
	  those needs an external event, so a node that reboots is left with no
	  clock at all until one happens to arrive. In the field that can be a
	  long time, and everything epoch-stamped in between is stamped with
	  nothing.

	  Two things are needed and both are already here: retained RAM that
	  survives the reset (the same mechanism the boot log uses, measured
	  surviving `west flash`, a software reboot and an image swap), and a
	  counter that keeps running across it to measure the gap.

	  It restores at DEVICE quality, NOT at the quality the time originally
	  had, and that is deliberate — see the note in the source. Restoring at
	  the original quality would let a remembered time outrank a live source.

	  A true power cycle clears retained RAM, so this covers warm resets only:
	  watchdog, software reboot, fault.

	  PROVEN ON HARDWARE 2026-08-29, on a V4 with a GNSS fix:

	    clock: epoch 1788049005, source GPS
	           restored across the reset (12966 ms gap)

	  The reported source being GPS is the point rather than an inconsistency:
	  the clock was restored at DEVICE quality and a live GNSS fix then replaced
	  it, which is exactly what restoring at the ORIGINAL quality would have
	  prevented.

	  ⚠️ It took two attempts, and the first is worth recording. The first image
	  carrying this did not survive its test boot — it took a watchdog reset and
	  MCUboot reverted it. The obvious suspect, that this option's `select
	  COUNTER` newly initialises the counter driver, was checked and is WRONG:
	  COUNTER and COUNTER_RTC_ESP32 are already y with this option off, so the
	  select changes nothing. The IDENTICAL image then booted, saved and restored
	  correctly on the next attempt, so that failure was a reset from some other
	  source landing inside the test-boot window; this bench has a fault that
	  panics a node every few hours (agents-quo6), which is the likeliest
	  candidate.

	  The lesson is about evidence rather than about this feature: one reverted
	  test boot does not identify what caused the reset, and reasoning backwards
	  from it produced a confident wrong diagnosis.

if MESHTASTIC_CLOCK_PERSIST

config MESHTASTIC_CLOCK_PERSIST_INTERVAL_S
	int "Seconds between saves"
	default 30
	range 1 3600
	help
	  How often the epoch and the counter reading are written to retained
	  RAM. They are saved together, so a stale save costs nothing in
	  accuracy — the downtime is measured from the counter, not from how
	  recent the save was. The interval only bounds how much of a live
	  clock's progress is lost if the node dies between saves.

config MESHTASTIC_CLOCK_PERSIST_MAX_DOWNTIME_S
	int "Refuse to restore across a gap longer than this"
	default 300
	range 1 28800
	help
	  A sanity bound on the measured downtime, and the guard that makes the
	  counter's 32-bit wrap a non-issue.

	  A warm reset takes seconds. A computed downtime of hours therefore means
	  something is wrong — the counter wrapped, or the saved record is garbage
	  that happened to pass its magic check — and the honest response is to
	  decline rather than to seed the clock with a confident wrong answer.

	  The counter wraps every ~8.8 h at this SoC's RTC frequency. An outage
	  that long would have cleared retained RAM along with everything else, so
	  a wrapped delta cannot be a real downtime; this bound rejects it.

endif # MESHTASTIC_CLOCK_PERSIST
